## SQL injection (critical)

`req.params.id` is interpolated straight into the query string. A request to `/user/1 OR 1=1` dumps every user; `/user/1; DROP TABLE users--` is available if your driver allows multiple statements. Use a parameterized query:

```js
db.query('SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE id = ?', [req.params.id], cb);
```

The placeholder syntax depends on your driver — `?` for mysql/mysql2, `$1` for node-postgres. Note that parameterization protects values, not identifiers; table and column names can never be parameterized.

## The error argument is discarded

`(e, r) => res.json(r)` ignores `e` entirely. When the query fails, `r` is `undefined` and you send `200` with a body of `undefined` — the client sees success. Callers will parse that as a valid response.

```js
if (e) return next(e);
```

Passing to `next` routes it to your error handler rather than leaking driver messages (which often include the failing SQL) to the client.

## Missing row means 200, not 404

For an id that doesn't exist, most drivers return an empty array, so the client gets `200 []`. A single-resource route should distinguish "no such user":

```js
if (!rows.length) return res.sendStatus(404);
res.json(rows[0]);
```

Also note `res.json(rows)` returns an array for what is semantically one object — `rows[0]` is almost certainly what you want.

## `SELECT *` leaks columns

Whatever is in the table goes over the wire: `password_hash`, `reset_token`, `stripe_customer_id`, internal flags. It's also fragile — adding a column later silently changes your API response. Name the columns explicitly.

## No authorization

Nothing checks who is asking. Any caller can read any user by incrementing the id. You likely want either an auth middleware plus an ownership check, or a separate `/me` route for self-access and an admin-gated route for arbitrary ids.

