`event.waitUntil()` tells the browser to keep the worker alive until the promise settles; without it your caching work may be cut short. `event.respondWith()` is what actually hijacks the response.

## Gotchas worth knowing

- **Don't cache `sw.js` aggressively.** Browsers cap its cache lifetime at 24 hours, but a long `Cache-Control` max-age on it can still strand users on an old version. Serve it with `Cache-Control: no-cache`.
- **Updates are byte-comparison based.** The browser re-fetches the script on navigation and installs a new version only if the bytes differ, so version your cache names rather than relying on content hashes in the worker filename.
- **Scope can be widened** with a `Service-Worker-Allowed` response header if you must serve the script from a subdirectory.
- **Cache-first for everything will serve stale HTML.** Use network-first or stale-while-revalidate for navigation requests.

For anything beyond a toy, [Workbox](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/workbox) handles precache manifests, routing, and update flows so you aren't hand-rolling cache invalidation.

